# Code Comments Standard

This educational comment standard **overrides** the usual preference to avoid verbose comments in this repo.

When creating or editing source/config files, add comments in this style by default. Do not wait for the user to ask.

## Language

- Comments in **English only**.
- Use short, simple words.
- Explain purpose and intent, not a line-by-line rewrite of the code.

## Required: Main Responsibility header

Every source or config file must start with a clearly marked **Main Responsibility** block.

Java (class-level):

```java
/**
 * Main Responsibility: Create and validate JWT access tokens.
 *
 * Used by AuthService (issue token) and JwtAuthFilter (read token).
 */
```

JavaScript (file-level, top of file):

```js
/**
 * Main Responsibility: Shared HTTP helper for backend API calls.
 *
 * Adds JSON headers and Bearer token when present.
 */
```

YAML / Compose / env / Dockerfile (`#`):

```yaml
# Main Responsibility: Local multi-container setup (Postgres + backend).
```

XML (`<!-- -->`):

```xml
<!--
  Main Responsibility: Maven build and dependencies for the Spring Boot backend.
-->
```

JSON files cannot have comments — skip body comments; document nearby (e.g. vite.config.js).

## Method / function comments

Add a short comment (1–3 lines) when behavior is non-obvious, security-related, or project-specific.

**Comment when:** JWT build/parse, filter chain order, CORS, password hashing, 401 handling, proxy paths, config quirks.

**Skip when:** getters/setters, trivial DTOs, Spring Data method names that already say what they do (`findByEmail`).

## What not to do

- No TODOs, future-feature comments, or commented-out code.
- No Bulgarian in source comments.
- Do not comment every line inside a method.
- Do not change logic while only adding comments.

## Examples

```java
// GOOD — explains why, not what
// Derive a fixed-length key so short local JWT_SECRET values still work with HS256.

// BAD — restates the code
// Hash the secret with SHA-256.
```
